[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
### TL;DR  
**Insert your data layer *before* the first label (symbol) layer** – either by passing the label‑layer id as the second argument to `map.addLayer()` or by moving the layer later with `map.moveLayer()`.  
The trick is to discover the *first* symbol layer in the style programmatically so you don’t have to hard‑code a name like `road-label`.

---

## 1. Why the order matters

MapLibre draws layers **bottom‑to‑top** in the order they appear in the style’s `layers` array.

```text
layers[0] → drawn first (bottom)
layers[1] → drawn on top of layers[0]
…
layers[n] → drawn last (top)
```

If you call

```js
map.addLayer(myDataLayer);
```

without a second argument, the layer is appended to the end of the array, i.e. **above every existing layer** – including all the street‑name and POI label layers. The result is exactly what you’re seeing: your data hides the labels.

---

## 2. The “insert‑before‑first‑symbol” pattern

The most robust way to keep labels visible is to insert your layer **right before the first symbol layer** (the first layer that draws text or icons). Symbol layers are the ones that have `type: "symbol"` and usually a `layout["text-field"]` or `layout["icon-image"]`.

### 2.1. Find the first symbol layer (once the style is loaded)

```js
function getFirstSymbolLayerId() {
  const style = map.getStyle();
  // Guard against the style not being ready yet
  if (!style || !style.layers) return null;

  // Return the id of the first layer that:
  //   - is a symbol layer
  //   - has a text-field or icon-image (i.e. really a label layer)
  for (const layer of style.layers) {
    if (layer.type === 'symbol' &&
        (layer.layout?.['text-field'] || layer.layout?.['icon-image'])) {
      return layer.id;
    }
  }
  // Fallback: if no explicit label layer is found, just return the first symbol layer
  return style.layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol')?.id || null;
}
```

### 2.2. Add your layer *before* that id

```js
map.on('style.load', () => {
  const beforeId = getFirstSymbolLayerId();

  // Your data source (GeoJSON, vector tiles, etc.)
  map.addSource('my-data', {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: myGeojson
  });

  // The visual layer you want to draw
  const myLayer = {
    id: 'my-data-fill',
    type: 'fill',
    source: 'my-data',
    paint: {
      'fill-color': '#ff6600',
      'fill-opacity': 0.4
    }
  };

  // Insert it before the first label layer (or at the end if none found)
  map.addLayer(myLayer, beforeId);
});
```

If the style is already loaded and you added the layer earlier, you can **move** it after the fact:

```js
const beforeId = getFirstSymbolLayerId();
if (beforeId) {
  map.moveLayer('my-data-fill', beforeId);
}
```

---

## 3. Quick‑and‑dirty “hard‑coded” alternative (only if you know the label layer name)

If you’re using a well‑known basemap (e.g. MapLibre’s default “OpenMapTiles” style) you can look at the style source and pick a stable label layer name such as `road-label`, `waterway-label`, or `place-label`. Then:

```js
map.addLayer(myLayer, 'road-label');   // insert *below* road labels
```

**Caveat:** When you switch basemaps or the provider updates the style, that hard‑coded id may disappear, causing your layer to again end up on top. That’s why the programmatic discovery shown above is preferred.

---

## 4. Full example (self‑contained)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – keep labels visible</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any style you like
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12
  });

  // Dummy GeoJSON – replace with your own
  const myGeojson = {
    type: 'FeatureCollection',
    features: [{
      type: 'Feature',
      geometry: { type: 'Polygon', coordinates: [[
        [-122.45, 37.75],
        [-122.44, 37.75],
        [-122.44, 37.76],
        [-122.45, 37.76],
        [-122.45, 37.75]
      ]]},
      properties: {}
    }]
  };

  // Helper to locate the first label layer
  function getFirstLabelLayerId() {
    const style = map.getStyle();
    if (!style?.layers) return null;
    for (const l of style.layers) {
      if (l.type === 'symbol' && (l.layout?.['text-field'] || l.layout?.['icon-image'])) {
        return l.id;
      }
    }
    return null;
  }

  map.on('style.load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Add the source
    map.addSource('my-data', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: myGeojson
    });

    // 2️⃣ Define the visual layer
    const dataLayer = {
      id: 'my-data-fill',
      type: 'fill',
      source: 'my-data',
      paint: {
        'fill-color': '#ff6600',
        'fill-opacity': 0.4
      }
    };

    // 3️⃣ Insert it before the first label layer
    const beforeId = getFirstLabelLayerId();
    map.addLayer(dataLayer, beforeId); // if beforeId is null, it just appends (no labels to hide)
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page, zoom around, and you’ll see the orange polygon **under** the street‑name and POI labels.

---

## 5. Checklist – “Did I miss anything?”

| Step | What to verify |
|------|----------------|
| **Style loaded** | `map.on('style.load', …)` or `map.isStyleLoaded()` before calling `addLayer`. |
| **Source added** | `map.addSource('my-data', …)` before the layer. |
| **Layer `before` argument** | Pass the id of the first label layer (`symbol` with `text-field`/`icon-image`). |
| **If already added** | Call `map.moveLayer('my-data-fill', beforeId)`. |
| **Layer type** | Use a type that makes sense (`fill`, `line`, `circle`, etc.) – not `symbol` unless you really need text/icons. |
| **No duplicate ids** | Ensure your layer’s `id` is unique in the style. |
| **Re‑load handling** | When the user changes the basemap (e.g. `map.setStyle()`), re‑run the insertion logic because the layer order is reset. |

---

## 6. Bonus: Keep the order stable across style changes

If your app lets the user switch basemaps at runtime, wrap the insertion logic in a helper that you call **after every `style.load`** event:

```js
function injectMyDataLayer() {
  if (!map.getSource('my-data')) {
    map.addSource('my-data', { type: 'geojson', data: myGeojson });
  }
  const layerId = 'my-data-fill';
  if (!map.getLayer(layerId)) {
    const before = getFirstLabelLayerId();
    map.addLayer({
      id: layerId,
      type: 'fill',
      source: 'my-data',
      paint: { 'fill-color': '#ff6600', 'fill-opacity': 0.4 }
    }, before);
  } else {
    // layer already exists – just make sure it’s still before the labels
    const before = getFirstLabelLayerId();
    if (before) map.moveLayer(layerId, before);
  }
}

map.on('style.load', injectMyDataLayer);
```

Now, no matter which style you load, your data stays **under** the labels.

---

### TL;DR Recap

1. **Find the first label layer** (`type: "symbol"` with a `text-field` or `icon-image`).  
2. **Add or move your data layer** *before* that id (`map.addLayer(myLayer, firstLabelId)` or `map.moveLayer(myLayerId, firstLabelId)`).  
3. Run the logic after every `style.load` so it survives style switches.

That’s all you need to keep your GeoJSON (or any other source) from covering up street names and other labels. Happy mapping!

**Short answer:**  
No – a MapLibre *style* is not a data source and cannot be added with `map.addSource()`.  
A style JSON only describes *how* to draw data that lives in one or more sources (vector, raster, GeoJSON, etc.). To reuse a single layer from that style you have to:

1. **Pull the source definition(s) that the layer depends on** (the `sources` object in the style).  
2. **Add those source(s) to your own map** with `map.addSource()`.  
3. **Add the layer definition** you want (the entry from the `layers` array) with `map.addLayer()`, making sure the `source`, `source-layer`, and any other required properties match the source you just added.  

Below is a step‑by‑step guide, plus a few gotchas that often bite people when they try to “import a style as a source”.

---

## 1. Why a style JSON can’t be a source

| Concept | What it is | How MapLibre treats it |
|---------|------------|------------------------|
| **Source** | Tile endpoint, GeoJSON, raster image, PMTiles, etc. | Added with `map.addSource(id, sourceDefinition)`. Provides raw feature data. |
| **Style** | JSON that lists *sources*, *layers*, *sprite*, *glyphs*, *metadata*, etc. | Consumed by `map.setStyle(styleUrlOrObject)`. It tells the renderer *which* sources to use and *how* to draw them. |
| **Layer** | A single drawing rule (line, fill, symbol, raster, …) that references a source. | Added with `map.addLayer(layerDefinition, beforeId?)`. |

A style is a *recipe*; a source is an *ingredient*. You can’t add the whole recipe as an ingredient – you have to add the ingredients first, then the recipe steps you care about.

---

## 2. Extract the pieces you need

Assume the remote style looks like this (trimmed for clarity):

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "osm-basemap": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "https://example.com/tiles/osm-basemap.json"
    },
    "satellite": {
      "type": "raster",
      "tiles": ["https://example.com/sat/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg"],
      "tileSize": 256
    }
  },
  "sprite": "https://example.com/sprites/osm",
  "glyphs": "https://example.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "background",
      "type": "background",
      "paint": { "background-color": "#f8f4f0" }
    },
    {
      "id": "water",
      "type": "fill",
      "source": "osm-basemap",
      "source-layer": "water",
      "paint": { "fill-color": "#a0c8f0" }
    },
    {
      "id": "satellite",
      "type": "raster",
      "source": "satellite",
      "paint": { "raster-opacity": 0.8 }
    }
  ]
}
```

You want to reuse the **`water`** layer (or any other background‑type layer) on top of *your* own basemap.

### 2.1 Identify the source used by that layer

- `water` → `source: "osm-basemap"`  
- `source-layer: "water"` (required for vector sources)

If the layer you want is a **background** layer (`type: "background"`), it does **not** need a source at all – you can just copy the layer definition directly. If it’s a vector/raster layer, you must add the referenced source.

### 2.2 Copy the source definition

```js
// In your own map initialization code
map.addSource('osm-basemap', {
  type: 'vector',
  // Prefer the TileJSON endpoint (`url`) when it exists:
  url: 'https://example.com/tiles/osm-basemap.json'
  // If only a tiles template is available you could use:
  // tiles: ['https://example.com/tiles/osm-basemap/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf'],
  // minzoom: 0,
  // maxzoom: 14
});
```

*If the source uses `tiles` instead of `url`, remember to supply `minzoom`/`maxzoom` yourself; otherwise MapLibre will assume `maxzoom: 22` and request tiles that don’t exist.*

### 2.3 Add the layer

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'water',
  type: 'fill',
  source: 'osm-basemap',
  'source-layer': 'water',
  paint: {
    'fill-color': '#a0c8f0',
    'fill-opacity': 0.6
  }
}, /* optional beforeId */ 'road-label'); // insert below first symbol layer
```

*The second argument (`'road-label'` in the example) is the ID of the first label/symbol layer in your existing style. In practice you can find it programmatically:*

```js
const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol');
map.addLayer(waterLayerDef, firstSymbol.id);
```

---

## 3. What to do with the rest of the remote style

| Part of the remote style | What you need to do |
|--------------------------|---------------------|
| **`sprite`** | If the layer you are adding uses icons (`type: "symbol"` with `icon-image`), you must also load the same sprite (or a compatible one). You can either: <br>• Set `map.setSprite('https://example.com/sprites/osm')` **before** adding the layer (MapLibre 5+ supports `map.setSprite`), or <br>• Merge the remote `sprite` URL into your own style JSON before calling `map.setStyle`. |
| **`glyphs`** | Symbol layers that render text need the glyphs endpoint. If you already have a glyphs URL in your own style, you can keep it; otherwise add the remote one the same way (`map.setGlyphs('https://example.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf')`). |
| **`metadata` / `light` / `terrain`** | These are style‑level settings, not source‑level. If you want them you must merge them into your own style JSON manually. |
| **Other layers** | If you later decide you need more layers from the remote style, repeat the “copy source + copy layer” steps for each new source/layer. |

---

## 4. Common pitfalls (and how the skill “maplibre‑source‑wiring” helps)

| Symptom | Typical cause | How to fix |
|---------|---------------|------------|
| **Layer shows nothing, network tab shows 200s** | `source-layer` name typo or wrong schema. | Open the TileJSON (`url` endpoint) → look at `vector_layers` → copy the exact name. |
| **Tiles appear but are one zoom level off** | Raster source default `tileSize: 512` while the server provides 256‑px tiles. | Add `tileSize: 256` to the raster source definition. |
| **Icons are missing** | No `sprite` URL or the sprite files (JSON/PNG) 404. | Add the correct `sprite` URL, ensure CORS headers are present. |
| **Text appears in a generic system font** | `glyphs` URL missing or blocked by CORS. | Add a working `glyphs` URL; MapLibre falls back to system fonts only when glyphs fail. |
| **`setFeatureState` does nothing** | Features have no stable `id`. | Add `promoteId: '<propertyName>'` to the source (or `generateId: true` only if you’re okay with index‑based IDs). |
| **Layer hides all labels** | You added the layer *after* the label layers. | Use the second argument of `addLayer` to insert it *before* the first symbol layer. |
| **Blank map, but you see 200s for tiles** | CORS missing on the tile server. | Configure the tile server (or CDN) to send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). |

---

## 5. Minimal reproducible example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Reuse a remote layer</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Your own basemap – could be OSM raster, vector, etc.
        myBasemap: {
          type: 'raster',
          tiles: ['https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
          tileSize: 256
        }
      },
      layers: [
        { id: 'my-basemap', type: 'raster', source: 'myBasemap' }
      ],
      // optional: your own sprite/glyphs
      sprite: 'https://mycdn.com/sprites/basic',
      glyphs: 'https://mycdn.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf'
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  map.on('load', async () => {
    // 1️⃣ Fetch the remote style JSON
    const remoteStyle = await fetch('https://example.com/remote-style.json')
      .then(r => r.json());

    // 2️⃣ Add the source the layer depends on (if not already present)
    const remoteSource = remoteStyle.sources['osm-basemap'];
    map.addSource('osm-basemap', remoteSource); // usually just a TileJSON url

    // 3️⃣ (Optional) set sprite/glyphs if the layer uses symbols
    if (remoteStyle.sprite) map.setSprite(remoteStyle.sprite);
    if (remoteStyle.glyphs) map.setGlyphs(remoteStyle.glyphs);

    // 4️⃣ Find the layer you want – here we pick the first non‑background layer
    const layerToCopy = remoteStyle.layers.find(l => l.id === 'water');

    // 5️⃣ Insert it *below* the first label layer of your own style
    const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol');
    map.addLayer(layerToCopy, firstSymbol?.id);
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*What this does:*  
- Loads your own simple raster basemap.  
- After the map is ready, fetches the remote style, adds its vector source, copies the `water` layer, and injects it just under the first label layer.  
- If the remote layer needed icons or text, the `sprite` and `glyphs` URLs are also applied.

---

## 6. TL;DR checklist

1. **You cannot add a style JSON as a source.**  
2. **Identify the source(s) the target layer uses** (`source`, `source-layer`).  
3. **Add those source definitions** (`map.addSource`). Prefer `url` → TileJSON when available.  
4. **Copy the layer definition** (`map.addLayer`).  
5. **Insert it at the right position** (use the second argument of `addLayer` to stay below labels).  
6. **If the layer uses symbols**, also set the same `sprite` and `glyphs` URLs (and make sure they serve CORS‑enabled files).  
7. **Check CORS, tileSize, promoteId, and source‑layer spelling** if anything still looks blank.

Once you follow those steps, you’ll have the remote background (or any other) layer rendered on top of your own basemap, without having to replace the whole style. Happy mapping!

Below is a checklist‑style “debug‑first” guide that walks through every reason a **GeoJSON → setFeatureState** call can be a silent no‑op, and then shows a minimal working example.

---

## 1️⃣ Why `setFeatureState` can be silent

| Symptom | Typical cause (for a GeoJSON source) |
|---------|--------------------------------------|
| `map.setFeatureState({source:'parcels', id: …}, …)` does nothing, no console error | **The source has no feature IDs** – the source never knows which feature you are referring to. |
| `getFeatureState` returns `{}` | Same as above – the ID you passed never existed. |
| Paint expression never evaluates the `feature-state` key | The style layer is not looking at the same `source`/`source-layer` (for GeoJSON there is no `source‑layer`) **or** the expression is written incorrectly. |
| Hover works on some features but not others | The property you promoted is missing or not unique on the “missing” features. |
| Nothing happens even after you add `promoteId` | The source was added **before** you called `setFeatureState` and you never re‑added it (or you called `setData` later, which wipes the IDs). |

The **single most common missing piece** is **`promoteId`**. Without it MapLibre generates IDs only when you set `generateId: true`, and those IDs are just the array index – they disappear as soon as the data changes, so `setFeatureState` can’t find the feature you think you’re targeting.

---

## 2️⃣ Fix checklist

| Step | What to do | How to verify |
|------|------------|---------------|
| **A. Add the source with `promoteId`** | ```js\nmap.addSource('parcels', {\n  type: 'geojson',\n  data: parcelsGeojson,\n  promoteId: 'parcel_id'   // <‑‑ key in `properties`\n});\n``` | Open the Network tab → the GeoJSON request (if you load it via URL) returns a `features` array. In the console run `map.getSource('parcels')._data` and you should see each feature now has an `id` field equal to its `parcel_id`. |
| **B. Make sure the ID values are unique** | Duplicate `parcel_id`s will cause the later feature to overwrite the earlier one, and the state will appear to “jump”. | Run `new Set(features.map(f=>f.properties.parcel_id)).size === features.length`. |
| **C. Use the correct source name in `setFeatureState`** | The `source` argument must match the source ID you added (`'parcels'`). No `source-layer` is needed for GeoJSON. | ```js\nmap.setFeatureState({source:'parcels', id:'12345'}, {hover:true});\n``` |
| **D. Call `setFeatureState` **after** the source is loaded** | If you load the GeoJSON via a URL (`data: 'parcels.geojson'`), wait for the `data` event: `map.on('data', e => { if (e.sourceId === 'parcels' && e.isSourceLoaded) … })`. | In the console, `map.isSourceLoaded('parcels')` should be `true` before you call `setFeatureState`. |
| **E. Write the paint expression correctly** | Example for a fill layer: ```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"parcel-fill\",\n  \"type\": \"fill\",\n  \"source\": \"parcels\",\n  \"paint\": {\n    \"fill-color\": [\n      \"case\",\n      [\"boolean\", [\"feature-state\", \"hover\"], false],\n      \"#ffcc00\",\n      \"#3388ff\"\n    ],\n    \"fill-opacity\": 0.6\n  }\n}\n``` | When you hover a parcel, the fill should change from the default colour to `#ffcc00`. |
| **F. Reset previous hover state** (optional but recommended) | Keep track of the last hovered ID and clear it before setting the new one: ```js\nlet hoveredId = null;\nmap.on('mousemove', 'parcel-fill', e => {\n  if (e.features.length) {\n    const id = e.features[0].id; // already promoted\n    if (hoveredId !== null && hoveredId !== id) {\n      map.setFeatureState({source:'parcels', id:hoveredId}, {hover:false});\n    }\n    map.setFeatureState({source:'parcels', id}, {hover:true});\n    hoveredId = id;\n  }\n});\nmap.on('mouseleave', 'parcel-fill', () => {\n  if (hoveredId !== null) {\n    map.setFeatureState({source:'parcels', id:hoveredId}, {hover:false});\n    hoveredId = null;\n  }\n});\n``` | Move the mouse over parcels – only the one under the cursor stays highlighted. |
| **G. If you ever call `source.setData()` again** | Re‑add `promoteId` (it stays on the source) **but** you must re‑apply any feature‑state you still want, because `setData` clears all state. | After `source.setData(newGeojson)`, call `map.setFeatureState` again for the feature you want highlighted. |

---

## 3️⃣ Minimal reproducible example (copy‑paste)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Parcel hover with feature‑state</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style
    center: [-122.45, 37.75],
    zoom: 12
  });

  // Sample GeoJSON – replace with your own
  const parcels = {
    "type": "FeatureCollection",
    "features": [
      {"type":"Feature","properties":{"parcel_id":"A1"},"geometry":{"type":"Polygon","coordinates":[[[-122.45,37.75],[-122.44,37.75],[-122.44,37.76],[-122.45,37.76],[-122.45,37.75]]]}},
      {"type":"Feature","properties":{"parcel_id":"B2"},"geometry":{"type":"Polygon","coordinates":[[[-122.46,37.75],[-122.45,37.75],[-122.45,37.76],[-122.46,37.76],[-122.46,37.75]]]}}
    ]
  };

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Add source with promoteId
    map.addSource('parcels', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: parcels,
      promoteId: 'parcel_id'   // <-- this is the missing piece
    });

    // 2️⃣ Add a fill layer that reacts to feature‑state
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'parcel-fill',
      type: 'fill',
      source: 'parcels',
      paint: {
        'fill-color': [
          'case',
          ['boolean', ['feature-state', 'hover'], false],
          '#ffcc00',   // hover colour
          '#3388ff'    // default colour
        ],
        'fill-opacity': 0.6
      }
    });

    // 3️⃣ Hover handling (reset previous state)
    let hoveredId = null;
    map.on('mousemove', 'parcel-fill', e => {
      if (!e.features.length) return;
      const id = e.features[0].id; // already promoted
      if (hoveredId !== null && hoveredId !== id) {
        map.setFeatureState({source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId}, {hover: false});
      }
      map.setFeatureState({source: 'parcels', id}, {hover: true});
      hoveredId = id;
    });

    map.on('mouseleave', 'parcel-fill', () => {
      if (hoveredId !== null) {
        map.setFeatureState({source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId}, {hover: false});
        hoveredId = null;
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**What to look for**

* Open the page, move the mouse over the two parcels – they turn yellow.
* Open the console and run `map.getFeatureState({source:'parcels', id:'A1'})`. You should see `{hover:true}` while the mouse is over that parcel, and `{}` otherwise.

If you remove `promoteId: 'parcel_id'` the hover never changes colour, `getFeatureState` always returns `{}`, and no errors appear – exactly the situation you described.

---

## 4️⃣ Quick “debug‑your‑code” checklist

1. **Source definition** – does it contain `promoteId: 'parcel_id'` (or the correct property name)?
2. **Feature IDs** – after the source is added, `map.getSource('parcels')._data.features[0].id` should equal the `parcel_id` value.
3. **Source loaded** – `map.isSourceLoaded('parcels')` must be `true` before you call `setFeatureState`.
4. **Paint expression** – uses `['feature-state', '<key>']` inside a `case`/`match`/`coalesce` etc.; not `['get', …]`.
5. **Hover handler** – passes the **feature’s `id`**, not `properties.parcel_id`.
6. **State reset** – optional but prevents “ghost” highlights when you move off a feature.
7. **Data updates** – if you ever call `source.setData()`, re‑apply any needed state after the call.

---

## 5️⃣ Common “gotchas” that still cause silence

| Gotcha | Why it fails | Fix |
|--------|--------------|-----|
| Using `generateId: true` **and** also trying to promote a property | `generateId` overwrites any existing `id` with the index, so the promoted property is ignored. | Remove `generateId` and keep only `promoteId`. |
| `parcel_id` is a number in some features and a string in others | MapLibre treats IDs as strings internally; mixed types can lead to mismatched look‑ups. | Cast all IDs to the same type (e.g., strings) before feeding them to the source. |
| Calling `setFeatureState` from a UI event that fires **before** the map has finished loading | The source isn’t ready, so the call is a no‑op. | Wrap the call in `if (map.isStyleLoaded() && map.isSourceLoaded('parcels')) …` or wait for the `data` event. |
| The layer you are styling is **not** the one you query for hover (e.g., you query a line layer but style a fill layer) | The `id` you set belongs to a different layer’s features, so the fill never sees the state. | Query the same layer you are styling (`map.on('mousemove', 'parcel-fill', …)`). |
| The GeoJSON is loaded via `fetch` and you call `setFeatureState` **before** you call `map.addSource` with the data | No source → no state. | Ensure the source is added first, then call `setFeatureState`. |

---

## 6️⃣ TL;DR – One‑line answer

> **Add `promoteId: 'parcel_id'` to the GeoJSON source (or otherwise give each feature a stable `id`). Then call `setFeatureState({source:'parcels', id:<parcel_id>}, …)` after the source is loaded, and use a `feature-state` expression in the layer’s paint.**  

Everything else (layer order, CORS, glyphs, etc.) is unrelated to the silent hover problem. Once the IDs exist, `setFeatureState` works exactly as documented.
